5.2. Nutritional value for different animal species
Orthoptera, like other insects, are highly nutritious and contain high amounts of protein. Various grasshopper, katydid and cricket species are already used for raising pets and zoo animals and have been investigated for livestock feeding. Particularly, the availability of large quantities of dead locusts resulting from locust outbreaks make them a good potential feed for livestock, especially poultry. Locust meal has been proposed as a poultry feed since 1930s. Poultry are also a means to control locust and grasshopper populations. Since 2000, the development of aquaculture in Africa and Asia and the search for alternative sources of protein have led to feeding trials on catfish and tilapia using locusts and grasshoppers. Locusts and other Orthoptera used for livestock feeding are fed live (free-range chickens, pets, zoo animals) or dried and ground (broilers, fish). Sometimes they are boiled before drying.
